// The Bit stop-the-world World/Mutator registry (runtime/gc/gcworld.bit, #1575)
// driven through a deterministic, single-threaded script, as a real linked
// binary.
//
// WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHY SINGLE-THREADED IS ENOUGH. The registry's hard part
// is a set of atomic PREDICATES a collector and a mutator each evaluate — "is
// this slot stopped for my epoch", "must I stay put" — and a rendezvous built by
// scanning the first. Those predicates are pure functions of the slots' words,
// so their truth table is exactly reproducible from one thread by putting slots
// into each state with the same entry points a real thread would and reading the
// verdict back. That is strictly what the ABI §5 contract is: whether a
// collection ABANDONS or PROCEEDS depends only on those verdicts. The genuinely
// concurrent property — that two OS threads racing the Dekker pair never both
// proceed — is not reproducible here and is not claimed; it rides on the seq_cst
// ordering the atomics lower to, argued in gcworld.bit's header.
//
// THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST (ABI.md §5, #1562/#1433): a BLOCKED mutator must not
// stall a collection — `worldStoppedFor` returns true for it and `rendezvous`
// therefore SUCCEEDS. `rv_blk` below is that assertion; break the blocked case of
// `worldStoppedFor` and this line flips from 1 to 0. The mirror — a RUNNING
// mutator DOES stall it (`rv_run` == 0, an abandoned collection) — pins the other
// side, so neither a always-stopped nor a always-running mutation survives.
//
// Under BIT_GC=stress the REAL (the deleted native runtime's) collector in libbitrt fires at every
// safepoint. It does not touch this registry — which lives in the raw `[]i64`
// `world` slice held in `main` — but it proves the `@nosplit` registry bodies
// take no safepoint while that slice stays rooted through `main`.

// SECTION G IS THE #1650 REGRESSION GUARD, and it is the reason this file is
// worth more than its length. The registry used to key nothing: one process-wide
// `curMut` cell stood in for the deleted native runtime's `root` module's
// `threadlocal g_mutator`, so the SECOND
// OS thread to register read the FIRST thread's slot, returned early, and never
// claimed one. It was then invisible to both the rendezvous and the root scan,
// and its live function-value cells were swept — `_tests_/stress/chanracelinux`
// faulted 20 runs out of 20 against a Bit-built `libbitrt.a`.
//
// NOT ONE ASSERTION IN THE WHOLE SUITE COULD SEE THAT, which is the part worth
// stating. The symptom is a branch through a freed `(code, env)` cell, i.e. a
// SIGSEGV/SIGBUS in an unrelated function on a stripped binary — so every test
// either passed or died somewhere that named the wrong culprit. The defect is
// only observable in the registry itself, so section G reads it directly:
// two distinct tokens must resolve to two DISTINCT slots, and each must resolve
// back to its own.
//
// Two tokens rather than two real OS threads, deliberately. What `gcthread.bit`
// supplies is a per-OS-thread VALUE; that it genuinely differs per thread was
// measured on hardware on all three targets (see that file's table). What the
// registry owes on top of that is that distinct values are kept distinct, and
// that is a pure function of the slot words — reproducible exactly from one
// thread, the same argument this file's header already makes for the predicates.
import {
  worldWords, worldClaim, worldRelease, worldSlotState, worldStoppedFor,
  worldBeginBlocking, worldRendezvous, worldRestart, worldEndBlocking,
  worldResumeGiveUp, worldClaimFor, worldFindOwner, worldSlotOwner,
  worldHighWaterMark, worldBlockAddr, worldEnsureSelf, mRunning,
  gcThreadToken, gcThreadEnter, gcThreadExit,
} from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { worldBeginSyscall, worldEndSyscall, worldSyscallSp } from "../../../runtime/gc"
import { wordAt } from "../../../runtime/gc"

// THE REGISTRY BLOCK IS MODULE STATE AND MUST NOT GO BACK ON THE GC HEAP (#1991).
//
// It was `let world = []i64(worldWords)` in `main`, and that is what #1991 was:
// section G below calls `worldBind(int(w))`, which installs this block as the
// PROCESS-WIDE registry that the live runtime's own safepoint poll reads. A
// managed slice is collectable and dies with `main`; the binding is a raw `int`
// the collector cannot see and nothing ever unbinds it. So every assertion below
// printed correctly and the process then took a SIGSEGV at teardown, reading a
// freed span. Holding `world` live through all of `main` does NOT fix it —
// measured — because the fault is after `main` returns.
//
// That also explains why #1991 read as "the block's SIZE breaks it": the size
// only decided whether a collection happened before teardown. Under
// `BIT_GC=stress` it broke from 2048 words, and under the default policy it was
// NOT monotonic (2600 crashed, 2688 did not, 2816 crashed) — the shape of a
// timing bug, never of a threshold.
//
// Module state outlives `main`, is never collected, and is where `runtime/root`
// keeps the real one for exactly this reason (`root.bit`'s `worldBlock`).
//
// SIZED 4357, NOT `worldWords`, DELIBERATELY. 4357 is `maxMutators * 17 + 5`,
// the widest slot anyone has proposed (#1904's saved register file), so this
// test keeps exercising the size that used to break and a future widening needs
// no edit here. An array length must be a literal, so it cannot track
// `worldWords` directly — `fits` below is the guard against it falling behind.
let worldStatic: [4357]i64

fn b2i(b: bool): int {
  if (b) {
    return 1
  }
  return 0
}

fn main() {
  // Zeroed by construction, which is the registry's expected initial state
  // (every slot free, no stop, epoch 0).
  let w = ptrOf(worldStatic)

  // The literal above cannot be written in terms of `worldWords`, so assert the
  // relation instead — the same shape as `rootInit`'s literal-vs-const guard.
  // A registry wider than its block would corrupt whatever follows it.
  print("fits ${b2i(worldWords <= 4357)}\n")

  // A — registration. Slots hand out in order and start `running` (1); release
  // returns a slot to `free` (0) and it is reclaimed.
  let c0 = worldClaim(w)
  let c1 = worldClaim(w)
  print("claim ${c0} ${c1}\n")
  print("state0 ${worldSlotState(w, 0)}\n")
  worldRelease(w, 0)
  print("free0 ${worldSlotState(w, 0)}\n")
  let c2 = worldClaim(w)
  print("reclaim ${c2}\n")
  worldRelease(w, 0)
  worldRelease(w, 1)

  // F — the collector's per-slot verdict (`worldStoppedFor`), the truth table
  // rendezvous is built from. running -> not stopped; blocked and free -> stopped.
  let a = worldClaim(w)
  print("sf_run ${b2i(worldStoppedFor(w, a, 1))}\n")
  worldBeginBlocking(w, a)
  print("sf_blk ${b2i(worldStoppedFor(w, a, 1))}\n")
  worldRelease(w, a)
  print("sf_free ${b2i(worldStoppedFor(w, a, 1))}\n")

  // B — a RUNNING other stalls the rendezvous: it never reaches a stopped state,
  // so the bounded wait expires and the collection is abandoned (false -> 0).
  let s = worldClaim(w)
  let o = worldClaim(w)
  print("rv_run ${b2i(worldRendezvous(w, s))}\n")
  // Abandon leaves the stop flag set (the caller clears it); do so before reuse.
  worldRestart(w)

  // C — a BLOCKED other is passed without waiting, so the SAME rendezvous now
  // succeeds (true -> 1). THE blocking contract, ABI.md §5.
  worldBeginBlocking(w, o)
  print("rv_blk ${b2i(worldRendezvous(w, s))}\n")

  // D — leaving `blocked` after the stop clears resumes to `running` (1) without
  // spinning. (Leaving while a stop is still pending correctly blocks instead;
  // that path is concurrent and is not driven here.)
  worldRestart(w)
  worldEndBlocking(w, o)
  print("resumed ${worldSlotState(w, o)}\n")

  // E — THE SYSCALL CONTRACT (#1904). A thread parked in a syscall runs no Bit
  // code but still holds live references, so it must be passed like `blocked`
  // AND scanned unlike it. These four lines are the mutation guards for that:
  //
  //   sys_state  the state is published (4, `mSyscall`) — not `blocked` (3),
  //              which would mean the collector skips the stack.
  //   sys_sf     `worldStoppedFor` passes it. Make this false and a subprocess
  //              wait stalls every collection to `stwSpinBound` again, which is
  //              the 21-abandoned-per-completed measurement this ticket opened on.
  //   sys_rv     the same rendezvous that `rv_run` shows ABANDONING (0) now
  //              SUCCEEDS (1) against a syscall-parked other. This is the pair
  //              that matters: `rv_run` 0 and `sys_rv` 1 together mean the state
  //              is doing the work, not that everything is passed unconditionally.
  //   sys_sp     the published stack pointer reads back while in the state, and
  //              reads 0 once out of it. A bound that outlived its state would
  //              send a conservative scan over reused stack words.
  //
  // Driven with a sentinel `sp` rather than a real one: nothing dereferences it
  // here, and a fixed value is what makes the read-back assertion exact.
  worldRestart(w)
  let sysSp = 0x7000
  worldBeginSyscall(w, o, sysSp)
  print("sys_state ${worldSlotState(w, o)}\n")
  print("sys_sf ${b2i(worldStoppedFor(w, o, 1))}\n")
  print("sys_sp ${b2i(worldSyscallSp(w, o) == sysSp)}\n")
  print("sys_rv ${b2i(worldRendezvous(w, s))}\n")
  worldRestart(w)
  worldEndSyscall(w, o)
  print("sys_resumed ${worldSlotState(w, o)}\n")
  print("sys_sp_cleared ${b2i(worldSyscallSp(w, o) == 0)}\n")

  // G — KEYED REGISTRATION (#1650). See the header note above this file's
  // imports for why this section exists and what it is standing in for.
  //
  // The registry starts clean: sections A-D released every slot they took, so
  // slot 0 is free again. The high-water mark does NOT reset — it is monotonic
  // by design — so nothing below reads it as a live count.
  //
  // Two distinct tokens, deliberately unlike any real thread pointer so that a
  // stray match would be a bug rather than a coincidence.
  const tokA = 0x7A11ED0001
  const tokB = 0x7A11ED0002

  // Neither is registered yet: an unknown token resolves to -1, never to slot 0.
  // This is the assertion a "return the first slot you find" lookup fails.
  print("g_missA ${worldFindOwner(w, tokA)}\n")

  let ga = worldClaimFor(w, tokA)
  let gb = worldClaimFor(w, tokB)

  // THE DEFECT, STATED AS A NUMBER: two OS threads must hold two slots. Under
  // the old process-wide `curMut` the second registration was skipped entirely
  // and both threads shared one slot, so `g_distinct` would read 0.
  print("g_distinct ${b2i(ga != gb)}\n")

  // And each token must resolve back to ITS OWN slot, not merely to some slot.
  print("g_findA ${b2i(worldFindOwner(w, tokA) == ga)}\n")
  print("g_findB ${b2i(worldFindOwner(w, tokB) == gb)}\n")
  print("g_ownA ${b2i(worldSlotOwner(w, ga) == tokA)}\n")

  // Re-registering an already-registered token must find the existing slot, not
  // take a second one — a thread holding two slots is a thread the rendezvous
  // waits for forever, which is the wedge an inexact token produced.
  print("g_again ${b2i(worldFindOwner(w, tokA) == ga)}\n")

  // The high-water mark covers both slots, so the bounded search can reach them.
  // A mark that lagged would hide a live thread's slot and hand it a second one.
  print("g_hw ${b2i(worldHighWaterMark(w) > gb)}\n")

  // Release clears the OWNER before the state (`worldRelease`'s ordering note),
  // so a departed thread's token is unfindable and cannot be inherited by
  // whoever claims the slot next.
  worldRelease(w, ga)
  print("g_gone ${worldFindOwner(w, tokA)}\n")
  print("g_ownFree ${worldSlotOwner(w, ga)}\n")
  worldRelease(w, gb)

  // No stop ever failed to clear, so the deviation-5 "never cleared" counter is 0.
  print("giveup ${worldResumeGiveUp(w)}\n")

  // H — THE CURRENT-MUTATOR HINT CACHE (#1698), gcworldsync.bit's `currentMutator`.
  // Every prior section released every slot it claimed, so the registry is clean
  // here (section G's own comment already establishes this for its own start).
  //
  // `currentMutator`/`worldEnsureSelf` cache the last resolved slot in a single
  // process-wide `mutHintSlot`, re-verified against the registry's own atomic
  // owner word before ever being trusted — never a plain trust-the-cache read.
  //
  // FROM HERE ON THE REGISTRY IS THE LIVE ONE, not this file's private block.
  // `worldEnsureSelf` is by definition the PROCESS-WIDE door, so the only
  // registry it can answer about is `runtime/gc`'s own, and reading it back with
  // `worldBlockAddr` is now the only way to name that block — #2184 deleted
  // `worldBind` so nothing can substitute a different one. That is the point:
  // the substitution was #1991, and this test performing it was the bug.
  //
  // Everything above stays on the private block through the parameterised
  // entry points, which is the #1833 rule for a program driving a registry the
  // live runtime has never heard of.
  let lw = wordAt(worldBlockAddr())
  let realToken = gcThreadToken()

  // First call: no hint yet, falls through to the exact search-then-claim path
  // and registers this real OS thread. Second call: SAME token, so the hint the
  // first call set must hit — same slot, no re-registration.
  let h1 = worldEnsureSelf(0)
  let h2 = worldEnsureSelf(0)
  print("h_cachehit ${b2i(h1 == h2)}\n")
  print("h_owner ${b2i(worldSlotOwner(lw, h1 - 1) == realToken)}\n")

  // Corrupt the hint on purpose: release the slot it names WITHOUT going through
  // `gcThreadExit` (which would invalidate it), then hand that exact slot to a
  // foreign token. The hint now names a slot this thread no longer owns — the
  // #1650 shape. `h_foreign_reused` just confirms the setup landed where
  // expected (lowest free slot); the real assertions are the two after it: the
  // stale hint must NOT be handed back (`h_recovers`), and whatever slot this
  // thread resolves to instead must genuinely be owned by ITS OWN token, not the
  // foreign one (`h_recovers_owner`) — a blind hint-trust would fail the first;
  // the pre-#1698 no-cache code would pass both by never having a hint to trust.
  worldRelease(lw, h1 - 1)
  const tokForeign = 0x7A11ED0004
  let gf = worldClaimFor(lw, tokForeign)
  print("h_foreign_reused ${b2i(gf == h1 - 1)}\n")

  let h3 = worldEnsureSelf(0)
  print("h_recovers ${b2i(h3 != gf + 1)}\n")
  print("h_recovers_owner ${b2i(worldSlotOwner(lw, h3 - 1) == realToken)}\n")

  worldRelease(lw, h3 - 1)
  worldRelease(lw, gf)

  // I — EAGER REGISTRATION (#1677/#1699). `gcThreadEnter()`, called with NO
  // prior door touch (no allocation, no safepoint poll — this thread has
  // released every slot section H claimed, so it is currently unregistered
  // again), must itself claim a slot for THIS thread's real token. That is the
  // exact call `runtime/root/<os>/boot.bit`'s `workerBody` now makes as its
  // first statement, closing the window #1677 proved reachable: a raw OS
  // thread whose first statements read a live reference out of shared/module
  // state (with the other copy then cleared) before touching either door is
  // invisible to the rendezvous and the root scan until it does — #1677's live
  // reproducer (a raw `threadStart` child, `BIT_GC=stress` on an already-
  // registered peer) swept such an object 20/20 runs; adding one allocation
  // before the hand-off (a lazy door touch) flipped it to survive every time.
  //
  // This section cannot reproduce the SWEEP itself — that needs a second, real,
  // concurrently-allocating OS thread racing a live collection against this
  // process's actual linked registry, which is exactly what #1677's completion
  // comment records in full instead of committing (a raw-thread timing probe is
  // not a stable CI regression). What IS stable and worth pinning here is the
  // PRIMITIVE's own contract: called before any lazy door touch, `gcThreadEnter`
  // claims a slot for this thread, visible to `worldFindOwner` and in `running`
  // state — i.e. eager registration is not a no-op, and skipping it is exactly
  // the gap that left #1677's thread invisible.
  //
  // NOT ASSERTED HERE: that the new slot stalls a DIFFERENT rendezvous. Slots
  // `s`/`o` from sections B/C/D above are claimed and never released (by this
  // file's own design — `worldEndBlocking(lw, o)` leaves `o` `running` for the
  // rest of the program), so ANY rendezvous below this point stalls regardless
  // of `iSlot`, which would make that assertion pass unconditionally whether or
  // not `gcThreadEnter` did anything — confirmed by a mutation run: forcing
  // `gcThreadEnter` to a no-op left `worldRendezvous`'s stall verdict
  // unchanged, entirely explained by `s`/`o`. `i_post`/`i_running` below do not
  // share that flaw — they read `realTokenI`'s OWN slot directly — and a
  // mutation run confirmed both flip (1 -> 0) when `gcThreadEnter` is broken.
  //
  // EVERY VALUE BELOW IS COMPUTED BEFORE ANY `print` RUNS, DELIBERATELY. A
  // `print` that interpolates a value allocates (string formatting), and an
  // allocation is itself a lazy-registration door (`registerAllocator`, wired
  // into the SAME `w` this section shares via `worldBind`) — a `print`
  // between `gcThreadEnter()` and the checks below would silently re-register
  // this thread through the allocation door regardless of whether
  // `gcThreadEnter` itself worked, masking exactly the defect this section
  // exists to catch.
  let realTokenI = gcThreadToken()
  let preSlot = worldFindOwner(lw, realTokenI)
  gcThreadEnter()
  let iSlot = worldFindOwner(lw, realTokenI)
  let iRegistered = iSlot >= 0
  let iState = -1
  if (iRegistered) {
    iState = worldSlotState(lw, iSlot)
  }

  // Leave through the real exit door, same placement rule `workerBody` follows
  // (#1666): after this thread can no longer run more Bit code that matters to
  // this test, and before any further slot inspection.
  gcThreadExit()
  let postExitSlot = worldFindOwner(lw, realTokenI)

  print("i_pre ${preSlot}\n")
  print("i_post ${b2i(iRegistered)}\n")
  print("i_running ${b2i(iRegistered && iState == mRunning)}\n")
  print("i_gone ${postExitSlot}\n")
}
